    ]
   },
   "section": "hook",
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "The Army said he was too sick to serve. *So he rode 497 miles.*"
   },
   "captions": false,
   "speed": 1.4
  },
  {
   "id": "name",
   "text": "This is Charles Young.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Charles Young",
    "sub": "1864 to 1922"
   },
   "section": "who"
  },
  {
   "id": "born",
   "text": "He was born enslaved in Kentucky in 1864.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "cabin",
    "caption": "The Charles Young birth cabin, Kentucky"
   },
   "section": "story",
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1864"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "wp",
   "text": "In 1889, he became only the third Black graduate of West Point.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "wp",
    "caption": "Cadets' quarters, West Point"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "buff",
   "text": "He led Buffalo Soldiers, and became the first Black superintendent of a national park, at Sequoia.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "sequoia",
    "caption": "Captain Charles Young, Sequoia, 1903"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "mex",
   "text": "In 1916, in Mexico, he led a cavalry charge, and lost not a single man.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "major16",
    "caption": "Major Charles Young, 1916"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "war",
   "text": "When America entered the First World War, he was the highest-ranking Black officer in the Army.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1917",
    "sub": "America goes to war"
   },
   "section": "twist"
  },
  {
   "id": "refuse",
   "text": "Some white officers refused to serve under him. Then the Army retired him, for high blood pressure.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "wilson",
    "caption": "President Woodrow Wilson"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "ride",
   "text": "So in June 1918, he rode on horseback from Ohio to Washington, to prove he was fit.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "house",
    "caption": "His home in Wilberforce, Ohio"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "June 1918"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "no",
   "text": "Four hundred ninety-seven miles. The Army still said no.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "497 miles",
    "sub": "and still no"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "end",
   "text": "He died in 1922, in Lagos, Nigeria. A century later, the Army made him a general.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "funeral",
    "caption": "His funeral at Arlington, 1923"
   },
   "section": "legacy"
  },
  {
   "id": "close",
   "text": "They said he was not fit. He proved them wrong.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "colonel2",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
